1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,731
Welcome to Introduction to Neural Network Search.

2
00:00:02,731 --> 00:00:07,022
Over the next ten minutes, we will explore how computers can

3
00:00:07,022 --> 00:00:10,533
automatically design the very neural networks that power modern

4
00:00:10,533 --> 00:00:11,313
artificial intelligence.

5
00:00:11,313 --> 00:00:16,775
If you are new to the field of AI, you might imagine that human

6
00:00:16,775 --> 00:00:20,676
engineers build every neural network by hand, much like architects

7
00:00:20,676 --> 00:00:21,846
blueprinting a house.

8
00:00:21,846 --> 00:00:25,357
But as artificial intelligence grows more complex, designing these

9
00:00:25,357 --> 00:00:27,698
networks manually becomes a massive bottleneck.

10
00:00:27,698 --> 00:00:32,379
That is where Neural Network Search comes in, transforming how we build

11
00:00:32,379 --> 00:00:36,670
AI by letting algorithms do the heavy lifting of architectural design.

12
00:00:36,670 --> 00:00:41,581
To understand Neural Network Search, often abbreviated as NLS or NAS, we

13
00:00:41,581 --> 00:00:45,674
first need to look at how neural networks are structured.

14
00:00:45,674 --> 00:00:50,585
A neural network is made of layers of artificial neurons that process

15
00:00:50,585 --> 00:00:53,041
data, recognize patterns, and make decisions.

16
00:00:53,041 --> 00:00:56,724
Traditionally, human data scientists spend weeks or months experimenting

17
00:00:56,724 --> 00:01:01,226
with different layer sizes, connection types, and settings to find a

18
00:01:01,226 --> 00:01:05,728
network that performs well on a specific task, such as recognizing

19
00:01:05,728 --> 00:01:07,365
images or translating languages.

20
00:01:07,365 --> 00:01:11,867
Automated network design is the process of using algorithms to automate

21
00:01:11,867 --> 00:01:13,095
this trial-and-error phase.

22
00:01:13,095 --> 00:01:18,006
Instead of a human guessing which setup works best, a computer program

23
00:01:18,006 --> 00:01:20,871
systematically tests, evaluates, and refines different network

24
00:01:20,871 --> 00:01:21,280
architectures.

25
00:01:21,280 --> 00:01:26,191
This is useful because it saves countless hours of human labor, often

26
00:01:26,191 --> 00:01:30,693
discovers designs that humans would never have thought of, and can

27
00:01:30,693 --> 00:01:35,195
tailor a network specifically to a unique hardware device or dataset.

28
00:01:35,195 --> 00:01:38,060
In short, it is AI designing AI.

29
00:01:38,060 --> 00:01:43,118
When an algorithm sets out to find a great neural network, it needs to

30
00:01:43,118 --> 00:01:45,648
know what options are available to it.

31
00:01:45,648 --> 00:01:49,622
This brings us to our second core component: the search space.

32
00:01:49,622 --> 00:01:54,319
Think of the search space as a massive menu of building blocks and

33
00:01:54,319 --> 00:01:55,042
architectural choices.

34
00:01:55,042 --> 00:01:59,016
Just as an architect is restricted by building codes and available

35
00:01:59,016 --> 00:02:02,990
materials, a neural network search algorithm is limited by the search

36
00:02:02,990 --> 00:02:04,797
space defined by its creators.

37
00:02:04,797 --> 00:02:09,494
This space might include choices about the type of layers to use, such

38
00:02:09,494 --> 00:02:13,468
as convolutional layers for images or recurrent layers for text, the

39
00:02:13,468 --> 00:02:18,165
number of layers in total, how those layers connect to one another, and

40
00:02:18,165 --> 00:02:20,695
settings like filter sizes or activation functions.

41
00:02:20,695 --> 00:02:25,392
If the search space is too small, the algorithm might miss out on

42
00:02:25,392 --> 00:02:26,475
brilliant, innovative designs.

43
00:02:26,475 --> 00:02:31,534
If it is too large, the search process can take an impossibly long time.

44
00:02:31,534 --> 00:02:35,508
Defining a smart, well-structured search space is therefore one of the

45
00:02:35,508 --> 00:02:38,760
most important steps in setting up a successful search.

46
00:02:38,760 --> 00:02:44,238
Once we have defined our search space, we need a strategy to explore it.

47
00:02:44,238 --> 00:02:48,542
After all, if a search space contains millions of possible network

48
00:02:48,542 --> 00:02:52,846
combinations, we cannot possibly test every single one-it would take too

49
00:02:52,846 --> 00:02:54,802
much computer power and time.

50
00:02:54,802 --> 00:02:59,497
The search strategy is the method the algorithm uses to decide which

51
00:02:59,497 --> 00:03:01,454
network architecture to try next.

52
00:03:01,454 --> 00:03:05,367
There are a few common approaches used in the field.

53
00:03:05,367 --> 00:03:09,671
One is random search, where the system simply picks random architectures

54
00:03:09,671 --> 00:03:12,018
from the space and tests them.

55
00:03:12,018 --> 00:03:14,757
While simple, it is not very efficient.

56
00:03:14,757 --> 00:03:18,279
Another popular approach is reinforcement learning, where the search

57
00:03:18,279 --> 00:03:21,800
algorithm acts as an agent that learns from experience.

58
00:03:21,800 --> 00:03:26,887
It proposes a network, sees how well it performs, and uses that feedback

59
00:03:26,887 --> 00:03:29,625
to propose better networks in the future.

60
00:03:29,625 --> 00:03:33,147
A third approach uses evolutionary algorithms, inspired by biological

61
00:03:33,147 --> 00:03:33,538
evolution.

62
00:03:33,538 --> 00:03:38,233
The system starts with a population of random networks, keeps the best

63
00:03:38,233 --> 00:03:42,146
performers, modifies or mutates them, and breeds new generations of

64
00:03:42,146 --> 00:03:43,320
networks over time.

65
00:03:43,320 --> 00:03:48,228
Of course, to know which networks to keep and which to discard, the

66
00:03:48,228 --> 00:03:52,003
system must evaluate how well each proposed architecture actually works.

67
00:03:52,003 --> 00:03:53,891
This is called performance estimation.

68
00:03:53,891 --> 00:03:58,799
The most straightforward way to evaluate a network is to train it from

69
00:03:58,799 --> 00:04:01,819
scratch on a dataset and measure its accuracy.

70
00:04:01,819 --> 00:04:06,349
However, training a complex neural network can take hours or even days.

71
00:04:06,349 --> 00:04:11,257
If you have to do this for thousands of candidate networks during a

72
00:04:11,257 --> 00:04:13,900
search, the process quickly becomes prohibitively expensive.

73
00:04:13,900 --> 00:04:17,675
To solve this problem, researchers have developed clever shortcuts for

74
00:04:17,675 --> 00:04:18,430
performance estimation.

75
00:04:18,430 --> 00:04:22,961
Some methods use proxy tasks, meaning they train the network on a

76
00:04:22,961 --> 00:04:28,624
smaller subset of the data or for only a few epochs to get a quick

77
00:04:28,624 --> 00:04:30,134
estimate of its potential.

78
00:04:30,134 --> 00:04:33,531
Other methods use weight sharing, where different network architectures

79
00:04:33,531 --> 00:04:38,439
share learned parameters so they do not have to start from scratch every

80
00:04:38,439 --> 00:04:38,817
time.

81
00:04:38,817 --> 00:04:42,592
These estimation techniques allow the search algorithm to quickly screen

82
00:04:42,592 --> 00:04:46,367
thousands of designs, reserving full, rigorous training only for the

83
00:04:46,367 --> 00:04:47,500
absolute best candidates.

84
00:04:47,500 --> 00:04:52,943
As we wrap up our introduction to Neural Network Search, let us review

85
00:04:52,943 --> 00:04:54,199
the main takeaways.

86
00:04:54,199 --> 00:04:57,967
We learned that automated network design replaces manual trial-and-error

87
00:04:57,967 --> 00:05:01,316
with algorithmic discovery, saving time and uncovering novel

88
00:05:01,316 --> 00:05:01,735
architectures.

89
00:05:01,735 --> 00:05:06,759
We explored the search space, which sets the boundaries of what designs

90
00:05:06,759 --> 00:05:10,527
are possible, and looked at search strategies like reinforcement

91
00:05:10,527 --> 00:05:13,876
learning and evolutionary algorithms that guide the exploration.

92
00:05:13,876 --> 00:05:18,063
Finally, we examined performance estimation, which allows the system to

93
00:05:18,063 --> 00:05:21,412
efficiently evaluate candidate networks without spending excessive time

94
00:05:21,412 --> 00:05:22,668
on full training.

95
00:05:22,668 --> 00:05:27,273
By combining these three elements-a defined search space, a smart search

96
00:05:27,273 --> 00:05:30,204
strategy, and efficient performance estimation-Neural Network Search

97
00:05:30,204 --> 00:05:33,553
empowers computers to build optimized AI systems autonomously.

98
00:05:33,553 --> 00:05:38,996
Thank you for joining this lesson, and we hope you feel inspired to

99
00:05:38,996 --> 00:05:43,183
learn more about how artificial intelligence continues to evolve and

100
00:05:43,183 --> 00:05:44,020
optimize itself.

101
00:05:44,020 --> 00:05:48,520
In conclusion, Neural Network Search bridges the gap between human

102
00:05:48,520 --> 00:05:52,570
ingenuity and computational efficiency by automating the design of

103
00:05:52,570 --> 00:05:53,920
artificial intelligence architectures.

104
00:05:53,920 --> 00:05:58,420
As you continue your learning journey, remember that the core

105
00:05:58,420 --> 00:06:01,120
pillars-search spaces, search strategies, and performance

106
00:06:01,120 --> 00:06:05,620
estimation-work together to unlock smarter, faster, and more creative AI

107
00:06:05,620 --> 00:06:07,420
models for the future.
